# The rewriting of wren_typed_block's typed member toward a first-order
# program: its lets of the typed
# versions reduced by beta, so that each call of a let-bound recursion is a
# term of its own, and each recursion specialized at its call by the static
# argument transformation. Compiled with wren_typed_block, whose defines
# these lemmas name. The typed versions' unfoldings into the natives are the
# module wren_typed_unfolded.
#
# Termination of this module's saturation, derived and not proved:
# - wren_static_argument_fixpoint matches an application of
#   wren_fixpoint_by_value to a body of three parameters whose recursive
#   calls pass the third unchanged, applied to two arguments. Its right side
#   holds two recursions: the specialized one has a body of two parameters,
#   and the typed branch's body calls its recursion with two filtered
#   arguments, not with the third parameter unchanged. Neither is that
#   shape. So the instances are at most the matching applications in the
#   input graph.
# - Each let lemma merges a let of a typed version, (λx. M x) T, with M T.
#   It adds one application node over two existing classes into the let's
#   own class, and no class. The instances are at most the classes holding
#   a closure times the nine typed names (eight and the recursion).
# - wren_typed_branch_identity merges a marked member with the member and
#   adds no node.
# The classes are those of the input and the static argument instances'
# right sides, and those are bounded as above.

# A let of a typed version, `(λx. B) T`, is B with T for x. The left side's
# `X^(M @ X)` is the class of M holding a closure, so the side is M T, and
# the right side M T, the β rule reading the application. Only lets of the
# typed versions are reduced: the untyped member's lets, and every other
# application, are not read by beta. By η.
